  "narrative_body": "After two days with Xi Jinping in Beijing, the American president told reporters that pending arms sales to Taiwan were \"a very good negotiating chip\" with China — the most explicit statement by any US president treating Taiwan's security as tradeable since the Taiwan Relations Act was enacted.\n\nThe response from Taipei was immediate and total. Lai Ching-te issued a five-point public statement insisting Taiwan \"will never be sacrificed or traded.\" Joseph Wu, the national security council secretary-general, reinforced the message on television. Alexander Yui, Taiwan's representative to the United States, appeared on CBS. Lin Chia-lung, the foreign minister, invoked the Six Assurances as unchanged US policy. Karen Kuo, the presidential office spokesperson, issued a formal rebuttal treating the sales as legally mandated and noting their scale had \"repeatedly reached historic highs.\" It was the most coordinated sovereignty messaging operation of Lai's tenure — damage management, not precaution.\n\nThe immediate outcome offered some relief: no sales were cancelled, and the American president said he had made \"no commitments\" while a \"decision\" was coming \"soon.\" That qualifier is itself the problem. The end-of-May 2026 deadline for Taiwan's HIMARS payment — miss it and the letter of offer and acceptance lapses — now falls inside the president's self-imposed \"decision soon\" window. Against a backdrop of $20 billion in undelivered arms, the risk of delay has risen, not fallen.\n\nThe PLA's behavior during the summit provided a secondary signal. Taiwan's defense ministry reported just 2 air force sorties on May 13 and zero aircraft on May 16, against a baseline of 10 to 12 daily sorties in prior weeks. Naval vessels — 7 to 8 ships — held at elevated levels throughout. The air reduction tracked the summit dates precisely. The pattern is diplomatic management of optics, not a pullback; the post-summit air baseline is now the watch item.\n\nEven as it defended its security position, Taiwan worked to reinforce its democratic standing elsewhere. Lai delivered a video address to the Copenhagen Democracy Summit, citing China's April obstruction of his flight to Eswatini as evidence of \"consolidating authoritarianism\" and calling democracies to unite. The New York Times published details of the covert mission — a borrowed royal aircraft, satellite phones, only carry-on luggage to save fuel across a 15,815-mile detour — amplifying Taiwan's narrative in global media at precisely the moment Washington was treating its defense as a bargaining chip.\n\nThe summit also sharpened Taiwan's domestic divisions. Cheng Li-wun, the KMT chairwoman, called the outcome \"the greatest blessing to the world,\" said her April meeting with Xi had made her more convinced of cross-strait peace, and called on Taiwan to abandon its \"first island chain\" role in favor of what she called a \"chain of peace and prosperity.\" The Mainland Affairs Council accused her of echoing Chinese propaganda. The DPP's legislative caucus said she was speaking for Beijing. Chang Jung-kung, the KMT's vice chairman, separately met Wang Huning — the Chinese Communist Party's fourth-ranked official — at a cross-strait cultural summit in Beijing. Two Republican members of Congress, Tom Tiffany and Chris Smith, wrote Marco Rubio, the US Secretary of State, urging that Lai receive the same US travel rights as Ms Cheng, framing the asymmetry as a policy concern from within the American system itself.\n\nThese divergent framings — DPP: our patron is treating us as a bargaining chip, sovereignty must be defended; KMT: the summit is a blessing, peace is possible — are now the organizing poles of the November 2026 local election campaign. The DPP is betting heavily on that frame. Lai formally nominated Puma Shen, a 43-year-old first-term legislator who founded the Kuma Academy — Taiwan's civilian defense organization, which has trained around 25,000 people — as the party's candidate for Taipei mayor. Beijing sanctioned Mr Shen in 2024 as a \"die-hard Taiwan independence activist\" and accused him of separatism. Running him in a race the DPP has not won for decades, against KMT incumbent Mayor Chiang Wan-an, with no three-way split to help (the TPP has pledged not to contest KMT incumbents), signals the party intends to nationalize the race around cross-strait threat. The risk is real: Mr Shen led the DPP's failed 2025 recall campaign against 31 KMT legislators under the same \"resist China\" frame, and voters rejected it.\n\nTSMC's board approved a capital injection of up to $20 billion into its Arizona subsidiary as part of a $165 billion US investment plan, and posted first-quarter net income of NT$572.5 billion with a quarterly dividend raised to a record NT$7 per share. The Japan joint venture, JASM, turned its first profit — NT$951 million in Q1, reversing a NT$1.39 billion loss the prior quarter. TSMC Arizona earned NT$18.81 billion in Q1 alone, more than all of 2025 combined. At its Technology Symposium in Hsinchu, the company confirmed the A16 process on track for second-half 2026 production, with A13 and A12 processes following in 2029 and 25 finalized 2-nanometer customer designs already in place with more than 70 in development. The most advanced nodes through 2029 remain anchored in Taiwan.\n\nThe opposition's presidential impeachment vote, timed for May 19 — the eve of Lai's second year in office — will not succeed. The KMT-TPP coalition holds roughly 60 seats; removing the president requires 76. Even if that threshold were cleared, the Constitutional Court cannot hear the case: only 8 of its 15 seats are filled, and the opposition's own 2024 amendments require 10 justices for a quorum. Both sides know the arithmetic. The vote is a message, not a mechanism.",
